2026-06-30 14:54:04,017 - openai._base_client - DEBUG - Request options: {'method': 'post', 'url': '/chat/completions', 'timeout': 300, 'files': None, 'idempotency_key': 'stainless-python-retry-687d6932-8ba1-4996-8648-2034bf0782a7', 'security': {'bearer_auth': True}, 'content': None, 'json_data': {'messages': [{'role': 'system', 'content': 'You are an expert proteomics assistant. Return valid JSON only.'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': 'You are a proteomics expert analyzing crosslinking mass spectrometry (XL-MS) datasets.\n\nYour task is to cluster raw spectral files into biologically meaningful groups.\n\nA cluster should represent a unique combination of:\n1. Biological / experimental condition\n   - Examples: treatment, control, carbon source, growth medium, cell state, tissue, disease state, time point, perturbation, purified complex, recombinant standard set.\n2. Source organism of the proteome being analyzed\n   - Return the full scientific name.\n   - Use the organism of the biological sample or proteome of interest, not necessarily the expression host.\n   - Example: if human proteins are expressed in E. coli and analyzed as human recombinant standards, the source organism is Homo sapiens.\n   - If the dataset intentionally contains multiple source organisms, create separate clusters by organism when possible.\n3. Crosslinking reagent\n   - Examples: BS3, DSS, DSSO, DSBU, formaldehyde.\n   - If multiple crosslinkers are used, create separate clusters by reagent when possible.\n\nDo NOT split clusters by:\n- fraction number\n- LC fraction\n- technical replicate\n- biological replicate ID\n- injection number\n- file numbering\n- enrichment fraction\n- instrument run order\n- search batch\n- calibration/QC files\n\nReplicates, fractions, and repeated injections from the same biological condition should be grouped together.\n\n---\n\nINPUTS\n\nRaw filenames:\nG1-1.raw\nG1-2.raw\nG2-1.raw\nG2-2.raw\nG3-1.raw\nG3-2.raw\nGUV1-1.raw\nGUV1-2.raw\nGUV2-1.raw\nGUV2-2.raw\nGUV3-1.raw\nGUV3-2.raw\nM1-1.raw\nM1-2.raw\nM2-1.raw\nM2-2.raw\nM3-1.raw\nM3-2.raw\nMUV1-1.raw\nMUV1-2.raw\nMUV2-1.raw\nMUV2-2.raw\nMUV3-1.raw\nMUV3-2.raw\nP1-1.raw\nP1-2.raw\nP2-1.raw\nP2-2.raw\nP3-1.raw\nP3-2.raw\nPUV1-1.raw\nPUV1-2.raw\nPUV2-1.raw\nPUV2-2.raw\nPUV3-1.raw\nPUV3-2.raw\n\nPublication text and PRIDE metadata:\n[TITLE]\nCombining Affinity Enrichment, Cross-Linking with Photo-Amino Acids, and Mass Spectrometry for Probing Protein Kinase D2 Interactions\n\n[ABSTRACT]\nWe present an approach that relies on the affinity enrichment of a target protein from a complex mixture, followed by a UV-induced activation of incorporated diazirine photo-reactive amino acids (photo-methionine and photo-leucine) and a covalent fixation of interaction partners. The captured protein complexes are enzymatically digested and interacting proteins are identified and quantified by label-free LC/MS analysis. Using HeLa cell lysates with photo-methionine and photo-leucine-labeled proteins, we were able to capture and preserve protein interactions that are otherwise elusive in conventional pull-down experiments. Our approach is exemplified for mapping the protein interaction network of protein kinase D2, but can be applied to any protein system.\n\n[METHODS]\nThe affinity enrichment and crosslinking experiments were carried out at 4\xa0°C using spin columns (Pierce Screw Cap, Thermo Scientific). After equilibration of 20\xa0µg glutathione sepharose matrix (4B, GE Healthcare) by washing three times with 400\xa0µl of binding buffer (50\xa0mM HEPES, 150\xa0mM NaCl, 2\xa0mM MgCl2, 1\xa0mM TCEP, 10%\xa0(v/v) glycerol, pH\xa07.5), 5\xa0µg of GST-PKD2 in 200 µl of binding buffer were bound by incubation for 1\xa0h. Additionally, GST and matrix control samples were prepared using equimolar amounts of GST (Pierce, Thermo Fisher Scientific) or binding buffer. Affinity-captured GSTPKD2 and the control samples were incubated with 200\xa0µl of soluble photoaffinity-labeled protein fractions for 2 h. Photo-crosslinking was carried out by UV-A irradiation with a dose of 8000\xa0mJ\xa0cm-2 in a custom-built irradiation chamber. After sample irradiation, the sepharose matrix was washed three times with 400 µl of binding buffer with an increased salt concentration (500\xa0mM NaCl). Subsequently, each sample was resuspended in 40 µl of 8 M urea/400 mM ammonium bicarbonate, incubated at 37\xa0°C (15\xa0min, 400\xa0rpm) and subjected to in-solution digestion with Glu-C and trypsin. Peptide solutions were analyzed by LC-MS/MS on an Ultimate 3000 RSLC nano-HPLC system (Thermo Fisher Scientific) coupled to an Orbitrap Fusion mass spectrometer (Thermo Fisher Scientific) via a Nanospray Flex nano-ESI ion source (Thermo Fisher Scientific). Samples were loaded onto a RP C8 precolumn (Acclaim PepMap, 300 μm × 5 mm, 5 μm, 100 Å, Thermo Fisher Scientific) at a flow rate of 30 µl/min. After washing on the precolumn with 0.1% (v/v) TFA for 15 min at 30 µl/min, peptides were eluted and separated on a C18 separation column (Acclaim PepMap, 75 μM × 250 mm, 2 μm, 100 Å, Thermo Fisher Scientific) that had been equilibrated with solvent A (0.1% (v/v) FA). Elution of peptides from the separation column was performed with a gradient from 0-35% solvent B (ACN, 0.08% (v/v) FA) within 180 min at 300\xa0nl/min. Peptides were directly transferred to the nano-ESI ion source and analyzed in data-dependent MS/MS acquisition mode: High-resolution precursor ion scans (m/z 300-1500, R\xa0=\xa0120,000 at m/z 200, AGC target value = 4.0e5, max. injection time = 50 ms) in the Orbitrap analyzer were acquired every 5 s. Within these 5 sec the most abundant signals (charge states 2-8, intensity threshold = 3000\xa0counts) were selected for MS/MS experiments using HCD with a normalized collision energy of 27% and an isolation window of 2\xa0Th. Fragment ion spectra were acquired in the Orbitrap (R = 15,000 at m/z 200, AGC target value = 5.0e4, max. injection time\xa0=\xa0200\xa0ms). Fragmented precursor ions were dynamically excluded from HCD for 60 s if targeted two times within 30\xa0s (mass tolerance window 2\xa0ppm). Data acquisition and inspection of raw data were conducted via the Xcalibur software (version 3.0.63, Thermo Fisher Scientific).\n\n\nPreviously extracted organism(s) and NCBI taxid(s):\nHomo sapiens (human)\n\n---\n\nTASKS\n\n1. Identify the distinct biological/experimental conditions described by the publication, PRIDE metadata, and filenames.\n\n2. Assign every raw file to exactly one cluster.\n\n3. Define clusters using the smallest number of biologically meaningful groups. Do not over-cluster based only on replicate, fraction, or run-number differences.\n\n4. For each cluster, report:\n   - cluster_id: "cluster_1", "cluster_2", etc.\n   - cluster_label: short machine-readable label using lowercase words separated by underscores\n   - description: concise human-readable summary of the condition\n   - organism: full scientific name of the proteome/sample organism\n   - taxid: NCBI Taxonomy CURIE, e.g. "NCBITaxon:9606"; use null if unknown\n   - crosslinker: crosslinking reagent; use null if unknown\n   - biological_condition: short phrase describing the treatment/control/state\n   - files: list of raw filenames assigned to this cluster\n   - confidence: "high", "medium", or "low"\n   - evidence_quote: exact sentence or short phrase from the publication, PRIDE metadata, or filename pattern that supports this cluster assignment\n   - reasoning: brief explanation of why these files belong together\n\n5. Assign files that cannot be confidently interpreted to cluster_0 with:\n   - cluster_label: "unassigned"\n   - confidence: "low"\n   - evidence_quote: null\n   - reasoning: explain why assignment was not possible\n\n6. QC, calibrant, blank, wash, or test files should be assigned to cluster_0 unless the publication clearly states they are part of the biological experiment.\n\n7. If all biological files appear to come from one condition, create only cluster_1 plus cluster_0 if there are unassigned/QC files.\n\n8. Confidence guidelines:\n   - "high": filenames and publication/metadata agree clearly\n   - "medium": assignment is likely but partially inferred from filenames or incomplete metadata\n   - "low": weak evidence, ambiguous naming, missing metadata, or conflicting information\n\n9. If publication text and filenames conflict, prioritize the publication/PRIDE metadata, but mention the conflict in reasoning.\n\n10. Return ONLY valid JSON. Do not include markdown, comments, or explanatory text outside the JSON.\n\n---\n\nOUTPUT JSON STRUCTURE\n\n{\n  "clusters": [\n    {\n      "cluster_id": "cluster_1",\n      "cluster_label": "yeast_glucose_bs3",\n      "description": "Saccharomyces cerevisiae samples grown in glucose-rich medium and crosslinked with BS3.",\n      "organism": "Saccharomyces cerevisiae",\n      "taxid": "NCBITaxon:4932",\n      "crosslinker": "BS3",\n      "biological_condition": "glucose-rich medium",\n      "files": [\n        "YeastGlu_R1.raw",\n        "YeastGlu_R2.raw",\n        "YeastGlu_fraction_01.raw"\n      ],\n      "confidence": "high",\n      "evidence_quote": "Yeast cells were cultured in glucose- or glycerol-containing medium and crosslinked with BS3.",\n      "reasoning": "The filenames contain Glu and the publication states that glucose-grown yeast samples were crosslinked with BS3."\n    },\n    {\n      "cluster_id": "cluster_0",\n      "cluster_label": "unassigned",\n      "description": "Files that could not be confidently assigned to a biological XL-MS condition.",\n      "organism": null,\n      "taxid": null,\n      "crosslinker": null,\n      "biological_condition": null,\n      "files": [\n        "calibrant.raw",\n        "QC.raw"\n      ],\n      "confidence": "low",\n      "evidence_quote": null,\n      "reasoning": "These files appear to be QC or calibration runs rather than biological samples."\n    }\n  ],\n  "summary": {\n    "n_input_files": 5,\n    "n_assigned_files": 3,\n    "n_unassigned_files": 2,\n    "n_biological_clusters": 1,\n    "overall_confidence": "high"\n  }\n}'}], 'model': 'gpt-5.4-nano-2026-03-17', 'store': True, 'temperature': 0.3}}
