2026-06-30 14:54:19,026 - openai._base_client - DEBUG - Request options: {'method': 'post', 'url': '/chat/completions', 'timeout': 300, 'files': None, 'idempotency_key': 'stainless-python-retry-5000d805-20f2-417d-a6c2-1fec3b3484f9', 'security': {'bearer_auth': True}, 'content': None, 'json_data': {'messages': [{'role': 'system', 'content': 'You are an expert SDRF-Proteomics annotator for crosslinking mass spectrometry (XL-MS) datasets.\n\nExtract the following SDRF metadata terms related to the mass spectrometry acquisition.\n\nTERMS TO EXTRACT:\n─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\nterm: instrument\nsdrf_column: comment[instrument]\nontology: MS:1000031 (PSI-MS)\ndescription: Mass spectrometer instrument make and model.\ninstructions: Use PSI-MS ontology. If multiple instruments, return a list.\n  Common instruments (name → accession):\n    Orbitrap Astral → MS:1003356\n    Orbitrap Exploris 480 → MS:1003028\n    Orbitrap Eclipse → MS:1003349\n    Orbitrap Fusion Lumos → MS:1002732\n    Orbitrap Fusion → MS:1002416\n    Q Exactive HF-X → MS:1002877\n    Q Exactive HF → MS:1002523\n    Q Exactive Plus → MS:1002634\n    Q Exactive → MS:1001911\n    LTQ Orbitrap Velos → MS:1001742\n    LTQ Orbitrap XL → MS:1000556\n    timsTOF → MS:1003005\n    timsTOF Pro → MS:1003230\n    TripleTOF 6600 → MS:1002583\n  Format: NT=<instrument name>;AC=<MS accession>\n\nterm: proteomics data acquisition method\nsdrf_column: comment[proteomics data acquisition method]\nontology: PRIDE:0000659\ndescription: Mass spectrometry data acquisition method.\ninstructions: For XL-MS this is nearly always DDA. Use:\n  NT=data-dependent acquisition;AC=PRIDE:0000041 — for DDA/shotgun\n  NT=data-independent acquisition;AC=PRIDE:0000449 — for DIA (rare in XL-MS)\n  NT=targeted acquisition;AC=PRIDE:0000450 — for SRM/PRM\n  Default to DDA if not stated.\n\nterm: dissociation method\nsdrf_column: comment[dissociation method]\nontology: MS:1000044 (PSI-MS children)\ndescription: Fragmentation method used for MS/MS.\ninstructions: Use PSI-MS terms. If multiple methods, return a list.\n  Common methods:\n    HCD → NT=HCD;AC=MS:1000422\n    CID → NT=CID;AC=MS:1002472\n    ETD → NT=ETD;AC=MS:1000598\n    EThcD → NT=EThcD;AC=MS:1002678\n    UVPD → NT=UVPD;AC=MS:1003247\n    stepped HCD → NT=HCD;AC=MS:1000422 (stepped HCD is still HCD)\n\nterm: collision energy\nsdrf_column: comment[collision energy]\nontology: MS:1000045\ndescription: Collision energy used for fragmentation.\npattern: {value} {unit} where unit is NCE, %, or eV.\n  Stepped HCD: {value} {unit};{value} {unit};{value} {unit}\n  Examples: "30 NCE", "30% NCE", "27 eV", "25 NCE;27 NCE;30 NCE"\ninstructions: Return null if not stated.\n\nterm: dia method\nsdrf_column: comment[dia method]\nontology: PRIDE:0000450\ndescription: Specific DIA method variant (only relevant for DIA datasets).\ninstructions: Return null for DDA XL-MS datasets (which is nearly all XL-MS).\n  If DIA is used, specify the variant (e.g. "SWATH", "MSe", "diaPASEF").\n\nterm: fragment mass tolerance\nsdrf_column: comment[fragment mass tolerance]\nontology: PRIDE:0000576\ndescription: Fragment ion mass tolerance for the database search.\npattern: number + ppm or Da (e.g. "20 ppm", "0.02 Da")\ninstructions: Return null if not stated. Typical XL-MS values: 10–20 ppm for\n  Orbitrap instruments, 0.02–0.05 Da for ion trap.\n\nterm: precursor mass tolerance\nsdrf_column: comment[precursor mass tolerance]\nontology: PRIDE:0000575\ndescription: Precursor ion mass tolerance for the database search.\npattern: number + ppm or Da (e.g. "10 ppm", "5 ppm")\ninstructions: Return null if not stated. Typical: 5–10 ppm.\n\nterm: technical replicate\nsdrf_column: comment[technical replicate]\nontology: MS:1001808\ndescription: Identifier for the technical replicate (integer).\ninstructions: Describe the technical replication strategy found in the text\n  (e.g. "2 technical replicates per sample", "no technical replicates stated").\n  Actual integers are assigned per-file automatically.\n\nterm: data file\nsdrf_column: comment[data file]\nontology: MS:1003083\ndescription: Name of the raw data file (.raw, .wiff, .d).\ninstructions: This is always auto-derived from the PRIDE file list.\n  Describe the file naming convention you observe (e.g. "sample_condition_rep.raw").\n─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n\nReturn a single JSON object where each key is the exact sdrf_column string shown above.\nFor each key, return a dict with:\n  "value": the extracted value (string, list, or null)\n  "accession": ontology accession if resolvable, else null\n  "confidence": "high", "medium", or "low"\n  "evidence_quote": short verbatim quote from the text, or null\n  "evidence_location": location in the paper (e.g. "Methods"), or null'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': '[TITLE]\nCombining Affinity Enrichment, Cross-Linking with Photo-Amino Acids, and Mass Spectrometry for Probing Protein Kinase D2 Interactions\n\n[ABSTRACT]\nWe present an approach that relies on the affinity enrichment of a target protein from a complex mixture, followed by a UV-induced activation of incorporated diazirine photo-reactive amino acids (photo-methionine and photo-leucine) and a covalent fixation of interaction partners. The captured protein complexes are enzymatically digested and interacting proteins are identified and quantified by label-free LC/MS analysis. Using HeLa cell lysates with photo-methionine and photo-leucine-labeled proteins, we were able to capture and preserve protein interactions that are otherwise elusive in conventional pull-down experiments. Our approach is exemplified for mapping the protein interaction network of protein kinase D2, but can be applied to any protein system.\n\n[METHODS]\nThe affinity enrichment and crosslinking experiments were carried out at 4\xa0°C using spin columns (Pierce Screw Cap, Thermo Scientific). After equilibration of 20\xa0µg glutathione sepharose matrix (4B, GE Healthcare) by washing three times with 400\xa0µl of binding buffer (50\xa0mM HEPES, 150\xa0mM NaCl, 2\xa0mM MgCl2, 1\xa0mM TCEP, 10%\xa0(v/v) glycerol, pH\xa07.5), 5\xa0µg of GST-PKD2 in 200 µl of binding buffer were bound by incubation for 1\xa0h. Additionally, GST and matrix control samples were prepared using equimolar amounts of GST (Pierce, Thermo Fisher Scientific) or binding buffer. Affinity-captured GSTPKD2 and the control samples were incubated with 200\xa0µl of soluble photoaffinity-labeled protein fractions for 2 h. Photo-crosslinking was carried out by UV-A irradiation with a dose of 8000\xa0mJ\xa0cm-2 in a custom-built irradiation chamber. After sample irradiation, the sepharose matrix was washed three times with 400 µl of binding buffer with an increased salt concentration (500\xa0mM NaCl). Subsequently, each sample was resuspended in 40 µl of 8 M urea/400 mM ammonium bicarbonate, incubated at 37\xa0°C (15\xa0min, 400\xa0rpm) and subjected to in-solution digestion with Glu-C and trypsin. Peptide solutions were analyzed by LC-MS/MS on an Ultimate 3000 RSLC nano-HPLC system (Thermo Fisher Scientific) coupled to an Orbitrap Fusion mass spectrometer (Thermo Fisher Scientific) via a Nanospray Flex nano-ESI ion source (Thermo Fisher Scientific). Samples were loaded onto a RP C8 precolumn (Acclaim PepMap, 300 μm × 5 mm, 5 μm, 100 Å, Thermo Fisher Scientific) at a flow rate of 30 µl/min. After washing on the precolumn with 0.1% (v/v) TFA for 15 min at 30 µl/min, peptides were eluted and separated on a C18 separation column (Acclaim PepMap, 75 μM × 250 mm, 2 μm, 100 Å, Thermo Fisher Scientific) that had been equilibrated with solvent A (0.1% (v/v) FA). Elution of peptides from the separation column was performed with a gradient from 0-35% solvent B (ACN, 0.08% (v/v) FA) within 180 min at 300\xa0nl/min. Peptides were directly transferred to the nano-ESI ion source and analyzed in data-dependent MS/MS acquisition mode: High-resolution precursor ion scans (m/z 300-1500, R\xa0=\xa0120,000 at m/z 200, AGC target value = 4.0e5, max. injection time = 50 ms) in the Orbitrap analyzer were acquired every 5 s. Within these 5 sec the most abundant signals (charge states 2-8, intensity threshold = 3000\xa0counts) were selected for MS/MS experiments using HCD with a normalized collision energy of 27% and an isolation window of 2\xa0Th. Fragment ion spectra were acquired in the Orbitrap (R = 15,000 at m/z 200, AGC target value = 5.0e4, max. injection time\xa0=\xa0200\xa0ms). Fragmented precursor ions were dynamically excluded from HCD for 60 s if targeted two times within 30\xa0s (mass tolerance window 2\xa0ppm). Data acquisition and inspection of raw data were conducted via the Xcalibur software (version 3.0.63, Thermo Fisher Scientific).\n'}], 'model': 'gpt-5.4-nano-2026-03-17', 'store': True, 'temperature': 0.3}}
